@page "/error"

@* Where an unhandled error lands outside Development: Program.cs re-executes a failed request here
   (UseExceptionHandler("/error")). Without this page the handler found nothing to render and the
   browser got an empty 500. It renders statically, with no interactivity and nothing from the
   content, so it cannot fail the way the page it stands in for did. *@

<PageTitle>Something went wrong &mdash; DungeonTable</PageTitle>

<section class="error-page">
    <h1>Something went wrong</h1>
    <p>
        The app hit an error while building this page. It is still running, and the table is as you
        left it: what was revealed, the fight and the quest log are all where they were.
    </p>
    <p>
        <a href="dm">Back to the DM screen</a> &middot; <a href="player">Player view</a>
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
        If it happens again, the app&rsquo;s log says what went wrong: look for
        &ldquo;An unhandled exception has occurred&rdquo; near its end.
    </p>
</section>
